<h1>Toolbars</h1>

<p>
    Configure the built-in toolbar with <code>ToolbarOptions</code>, or provide
    <code>ToolbarContent</code> to compose the library's control components yourself.
</p>

<h2 id="presets">// presets</h2>

<p>
    The default <code>BasicToolbarOptions</code> includes font, inline style, list, and link controls.
    <code>FullToolbarOptions</code> enables every built-in control.
</p>

<ExampleView TComponent="FullToolbarExample">
    <FullToolbarExample />
</ExampleView>

<h2 id="custom_content_and_fonts">// custom_content_and_fonts</h2>

<p>
    Supply <code>ToolbarContent</code> to choose and arrange controls manually. Custom fonts must also appear
    in <code>ToolbarOptions.Fonts</code>, and your application stylesheet must define the matching
    <code>ql-font-&lt;name&gt;</code> class.
</p>

<ExampleView TComponent="CustomToolbarExample">
    <CustomToolbarExample />
</ExampleView>

<h2 id="position">// position</h2>

<p>
    Set <code>ToolbarPosition</code> to <code>Top</code> or <code>Bottom</code>. The default is
    <code>Top</code>.
</p>

<ExampleView TComponent="BottomToolbarExample">
    <BottomToolbarExample />
</ExampleView>

<h2 id="hide_the_toolbar">// hide_the_toolbar</h2>

<p>
    Use <code>ToolbarOptions.None</code> when the editor needs no formatting UI. The equivalent custom option
    is <code>HideToolbar = true</code>.
</p>

<ExampleView TComponent="NoToolbarExample">
    <NoToolbarExample />
</ExampleView>

<h2 id="disabled_behavior">// disabled_behavior</h2>

<p>
    A disabled editor normally leaves a visibly disabled toolbar in place. Set
    <code>ToolbarDisabledBehavior.Hidden</code> to hide it while the editor is disabled. The
    <a href="components/rich-text-editor#enabled_state">enabled-state example</a> demonstrates both behaviors.
</p>
